'''IAA COMMISSION: FIRST REFERRALS — Justice Aoyama Announces Three Cases; Harujima, Kizan, Akamatsu Group; "The Teeth Are Biting"'''
IAA Commission Chair Aoyama Reiko announced the Commission's first three referrals: (1) Harujima — institutional abuse in the idol industry (ongoing); (2) Kizan — AI safety compliance audit (ongoing); (3) Akamatsu Group — corporate fraud and institutional abuse of employee pension funds (new). Aoyama: "The Commission has teeth. The teeth are biting. The Akamatsu Group case is exactly the kind of institutional abuse the IAA was designed to address — a powerful institution that exploited the people it was supposed to protect." The Mainichi notes: the IAA Commission's first three referrals cover the idol industry, AI safety, and corporate fraud. The scope is broad. The teeth are biting.

'''AKAMATSU GROUP: POLITICAL DONATIONS — Akamatsu Group Donated ¥48M to LDP Over 5 Years; POIA Investigation; "The Donations Were Legal. The Timing Was Not."'''
The Akamatsu Group's political donations are under review. SESC records show the Akamatsu Group donated ¥48M to the LDP over five years (2021-2026). The donations were legal — within the limits of the Political Funds Control Act. But the timing is under scrutiny: three donations totalling ¥12M were made within 30 days of SESC audit deadlines. POIA investigation: "The donations were legal. The timing was not. We are examining whether the donations were intended to influence audit outcomes." LDP spokesperson: "The LDP accepts legal donations from legal entities. The Akamatsu Group donations were legal and properly reported." The Mainichi notes: the donations were legal. The timing was not. The question is: did the donations influence the audits? And if so, how?

