'''EDITORIAL: The System and the Whistleblower'''
Two systems failed this week. Two whistleblowers did not.

The Akamatsu Group embezzled ¥18B from employee pension funds over three years. The SESC — the Securities and Exchange Surveillance Commission — received quarterly reports from the Akamatsu Group. The reports were falsified. The SESC did not detect the falsification. The routine audit did not detect the discrepancy. A mid-level accountant detected the discrepancy. A whistleblower — not the system — exposed the fraud.

The Ministry of Environment approved 14 waste export licenses without inspection. Former Deputy Director General Tanaka Hiroshi signed the licenses. He was told the inspections were completed. They were not. He did not verify. The system relied on trust. The trust was broken. Nobody in the system detected the failure. The blockade at Castejón detected the failure. The Baliscan government detected the failure. The Okaiken system did not.

The Mainichi notes: two systems failed. Two whistleblowers did not. The Akamatsu Group whistleblower — a mid-level accountant — had the courage to speak. The Baliscan government — a foreign government — had the courage to blockade. The Okaiken system — the SESC, the Ministry of Environment, the routine audits — did not have the courage or the competence to detect ¥18B in missing pension funds or 40,000 tonnes of uninspected waste.

The IAA Commission has teeth. Justice Aoyama has teeth. The first three referrals — Harujima, Kizan, Akamatsu Group — are the right referrals. But the Mainichi asks: how many more Akamatsu Groups are there? How many more Tanaka Hiroshis are there? The system failed twice this week. The whistleblowers did not. The system must be fixed. The whistleblowers must be protected. The workers must be compensated. The waste must be inspected. The system must answer for its failures.

'''SHIRATORI KENJI — The Hierarchy'''
The court appearance was brief. Twenty minutes. Three defendants. One dynamic.

Akamatsu Seijiro — silent. The chairman said nothing. The chairman who built the Akamatsu Group into one of Okaiken's largest conglomerates. The chairman who embezzled ¥18B from his own employees' pension funds. The chairman said nothing.

Ibuki Yuma — wept. The former entertainer turned executive VP. The younger brother. The man who used his fame to deflect regulatory scrutiny. The man who wept in court. The Mainichi notes: tears are not an explanation. Tears are not a defence. Tears are not accountability.

Izumida Shotaro — blamed. "I was following orders. The instructions came from the chairman's office." The CFO advisor. The former media commentator. The man who knew where the money went. The man who blamed the chairman. The Mainichi notes: "I was following orders" is not a defence. It is a confession. It is a confession that the hierarchy existed. It is a confession that the orders came from the top. It is a confession that the system was designed to exploit the workers and protect the executives.

The hierarchy is clear. The chairman gave the orders. The executive VP deflected the scrutiny. The CFO advisor moved the money. The workers paid. The executives stole. The system failed. The whistleblower spoke. The hierarchy is falling.

